# Make
Building not requires the following libraries. For linking, `TVM_LIBS` used `TVM_LIBS_LD_FLAGS`:
```
TVM_LIBS           = libtvm_runtime.a \
                     libtvm_ffi.a \
                     tvm_model.a
TVM_CPP_LIBS       = -Wl,--no-whole-archive \
                     -lstdc++ \
                     -ldl \
                     -lpthread \
                      /users/dcolange/local/lib/libbacktrace.a
TVM_LIBS_LD_FLAGS  = -Wl,--whole-archive
```
**IMPORTANT NOTE** - you will see libbacktrace hardcoded here. This dependancy seems to be missing on CRC machines, so I built and linked it and hardcoded it. This needs to be formalized for portability.

Not sure what `-Wl,--whole-archive` does but it is required for static linking of TVM libraries/models.\\\\
The TVM wrapper is build with the following options: `-c -fPIC -O3 -march=native`. For the compilation of this wrapper, the following libraries must be in `CPLUS_INCLUDE_PATH`:
```
* TVM     - tvm/include
* TVM FFI - tvm/3rdparty/tvm-ffi/include
* DLPACK  - tvm/3rdparty/tvm-ffi/3rdparty/dlpack/include 
```
`tvm_bindings.cpp` is compiled with the following flags:
```
TVM_WRAPPER_FLAGS = -c -fPIC -O3 -march=native
```
To test the TVM model, run `make benchmark_approximation.x` and `./benchmark_approximation.x`

# libxsmm
It seems that the update from the relay framework to relax removed support for `lixbsmm`. I think there is a way to use libxsmm to write optimized `C` GEMMs and directly write them into TVM.

Another option is to use compiled libxsmm to accelerate our `droplet_model.f90`. This accelerated performance by 3x per droplet. However, the second layer norm was broken, so the results were NaN, but the matrix multiplications dominate the runtime and the timings across the board seemed reasonable.
